{"parentUuid":"c19f7029-dc8f-4efb-963e-3178470de889","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_015UBiS8phRve4AeKGBdNs7e","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good question — my wording was misleading. Here's what actually happens with `TZ=UTC0`:\n\n```go\nfunc detectTimezone() string {\n    if tz := normalizeTimezone(os.Getenv(\"TZ\")); tz != \"\" { ... }      // 1\n    if link, err := os.Readlink(\"/etc/localtime\"); err == nil { ... }  // 2\n    if tz := normalizeTimezone(time.Local.String()); tz != \"\" { ... }  // 3\n    return \"UTC\"                                                        // 4\n}\n```\n\nWith `TZ=UTC0`:\n1. `normalizeTimezone(\"UTC0\")` returns `\"\"` (fails `time.LoadLocation`), so we don't return early — we **fall through to the next source**.\n2. On a typical macOS/Linux box, `/etc/localtime` is a symlink to e.g. `/var/db/timezone/zoneinfo/Europe/Berlin` → returns `\"Europe/Berlin\"`.\n3. Skipped.\n4. Only reached if all three sources produce nothing.\n\nSo in practice, a bogus `TZ` gets **silently ignored** and we use the machine's actual timezone from `/etc/localtime`. Literal `\"UTC\"` is only returned when every source fails — e.g., Windows with no `TZ` set, or a container with no `/etc/localtime` and an unconfigured Go `time.Local`.\n\nThat's arguably better than a hard UTC fallback (your local stats stay correct even if your shell has a weird `TZ`), but the commit message I wrote made it sound like `UTC0` → `UTC`, which isn't what happens. Want me to amend the commit message, or is the behavior fine as-is?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6,"cache_creation_input_tokens":190,"cache_read_input_tokens":183571,"output_tokens":1181,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":190,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","it