{"parentUuid":"29c2064a-dae9-4dbc-b68a-667cb8b9c3f0","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"db8e3be8-14f1-4029-8e32-dbbd15cff9f1","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":"If that is the intended product behavior, then the original review finding becomes much weaker.\n\nThe real issue is not “you must parse every timezone properly.” It’s this:\n\n- The code and commit message currently claim “only IANA-valid names reach the API” and that POSIX-style values like `EST5EDT` fall through.\n- The implementation does not actually enforce that. It accepts anything Go can `LoadLocation`, which includes some non-IANA aliases.\n- If the backend simply falls back to `UTC` for anything it doesn’t recognize, that may be an acceptable shortcut.\n\nSo I’d frame it this way:\n\n- If “best effort, else backend uses UTC” is the intended contract, I would not call this a bug.\n- I would call it a docs/tests mismatch. The comments are stricter than the behavior.\n- The only user-facing risk is mild mis-bucketing around day/hour boundaries when a non-IANA alias is accepted locally but not understood canonically by the API.\n\nPractical fix options:\n\n1. Keep the shortcut and update comments/tests to say “validated enough for Go; backend may fall back to UTC for unknown zones.”\n2. If you want stricter client behavior, reject anything that is not clearly an IANA name like `Area/Location` after stripping `:` and `.../zoneinfo/`.\n3. Push all normalization to the server and just send the raw candidate plus accept UTC fallback there.\n\nGiven what you described, I’d recommend option 1 unless the time-bucket accuracy matters a lot."},"uuid":"6667e107-5576-4143-bb74-62107b9b2f30","timestamp":"2026-04-22T15:55:41.880Z","permissionMode":"acceptEdits","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/soph/Work/entire/devenv/cli_experiments","sessionId":"ca23ad5c-2676-470b-99f5-98e30bac81e2","version":"2.1.117","gitBranch":"soph/stats"}
